/**
 * Glossary Batch 8 — Depth Expansions
 * Deeper coverage for: criminal, constitutional, contract, tort, property,
 *                      family, employment, immigration, bankruptcy, IP,
 *                      environmental, administrative, tax, cybercrime, international
 * ~220 terms
 */
const terms = [

  // ─── CRIMINAL LAW (EXPANDED) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  ['RICO (Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act)', 'A federal statute (18 U.S.C. §§ 1961-1968) that provides criminal penalties and civil remedies for acts performed as part of an ongoing criminal organization. RICO requires proof of: an enterprise, a pattern of racketeering activity (at least two predicate acts within ten years), and the defendant\'s connection to both. Predicate acts include over 35 designated federal and state crimes. Civil RICO allows private plaintiffs to sue for treble damages. RICO has been applied beyond organized crime to corporate fraud, political corruption, and insurance fraud.', 'criminal', 'Federal', ['Conspiracy', 'Organized Crime', 'Wire Fraud'], ['18 U.S.C. §§ 1961-1968', 'H.J. Inc. v. Northwestern Bell Telephone Co. (1989)']],
  ['Conspiracy (Criminal)', 'An agreement between two or more persons to commit a crime, combined (in most federal and many state formulations) with an overt act in furtherance of the agreement. Under the federal conspiracy statute (18 U.S.C. § 371), the conspiracy is the crime; the target offense need not be completed. Conspirators are liable for all reasonably foreseeable crimes committed by co-conspirators in furtherance of the conspiracy (Pinkerton doctrine). Withdrawal requires affirmative action to disavow or defeat the conspiracy before the target offense is committed.', 'criminal', 'Federal/State', ['RICO', 'Accomplice Liability', 'Pinkerton Doctrine'], ['18 U.S.C. § 371', 'Pinkerton v. United States (1946)']],
  ['Felony Murder Rule', 'A doctrine that imposes murder liability on a defendant who kills another person — even accidentally or through the acts of co-felons — while committing or attempting to commit an enumerated dangerous felony such as robbery, rape, arson, kidnapping, or burglary. The rule eliminates the need to prove intent to kill, substituting the intent to commit the underlying felony. Many states have limited or abolished the felony murder rule following criticism that it imposes disproportionate punishment on minor participants.', 'criminal', 'State', ['Murder', 'Accomplice Liability', 'Malice Aforethought'], ['People v. Anderson (Cal. 2021)', 'Model Penal Code § 210.2(1)(b)']],
  ['Mens Rea', 'The mental state or criminal intent required for conviction of a crime, Latin for "guilty mind." The Model Penal Code identifies four levels: purpose (conscious objective to engage in conduct or cause result), knowledge (awareness that conduct is of that nature or will cause that result), recklessness (conscious disregard of substantial and unjustifiable risk), and negligence (failure to be aware of a substantial and unjustifiable risk). Strict liability crimes require no mens rea for one or more elements.', 'criminal', 'Federal/State', ['Actus Reus', 'Strict Liability Crimes', 'Specific Intent'], ['Model Penal Code § 2.02', 'Staples v. United States (1994)']],
  ['Actus Reus', 'The voluntary physical act or unlawful omission constituting the objective element of a crime, which combined with mens rea forms the basis of criminal liability. An act must be voluntary — reflexive movements, hypnotic acts, or conduct during seizures are not actus reus. A duty to act may arise from statute, contractual obligation, special relationship, voluntary assumption of care, or creation of risk. Mere thoughts without corresponding conduct cannot constitute actus reus.', 'criminal', 'Federal/State', ['Mens Rea', 'Strict Liability', 'Omission Liability'], ['Model Penal Code § 2.01', 'Black\'s Law Dictionary']],
  ['Entrapment', 'A defense available when government agents induced the defendant to commit a crime they were not predisposed to commit. The subjective test (majority rule) focuses on whether the defendant was predisposed to commit the crime before government contact; the objective test (Model Penal Code) asks whether police conduct would have induced a normally law-abiding person. Evidence of prior criminal record or willingness to commit the crime rebuts the lack-of-predisposition element.', 'criminal', 'Federal/State', ['Undercover Operations', 'Due Process', 'Government Conduct'], ['Jacobson v. United States (1992)', 'Sherman v. United States (1958)']],
  ['Wire Fraud', 'A federal felony consisting of a scheme to defraud using wire communications (telephone, internet, email, radio, TV). 18 U.S.C. § 1343 requires proof of: a scheme to defraud, use of wire communications in furtherance, and materiality. Each use of wire communications is a separate count, enabling multiple charges per scheme. Wire fraud carries up to 20 years imprisonment (30 if affecting a financial institution or related to disaster relief). It is a RICO predicate act and frequently charged in securities fraud, health care fraud, and corruption cases.', 'criminal', 'Federal', ['Mail Fraud', 'RICO', 'Honest Services Fraud'], ['18 U.S.C. § 1343', 'McNally v. United States (1987)']],
  ['Brady Material', 'Exculpatory and impeachment evidence in the prosecution\'s possession that is material to guilt or punishment, which must be disclosed to the defense under Brady v. Maryland (1963). The prosecution\'s duty is affirmative — inadvertent or negligent non-disclosure is no excuse. Evidence is "material" under United States v. Bagley (1985) if there is a reasonable probability that disclosure would have produced a different outcome. The Giglio doctrine extends Brady to impeachment evidence affecting prosecution witnesses.', 'criminal', 'Federal/State', ['Due Process', 'Exculpatory Evidence', 'Prosecution Disclosure'], ['Brady v. Maryland (1963)', 'Giglio v. United States (1972)']],
  ['Money Laundering', 'The process of concealing the origins of criminally derived proceeds by passing them through a complex sequence of banking transfers, commercial transactions, or shell companies to make them appear legitimate. Three stages: placement (introducing funds into the financial system), layering (creating distance between funds and source), and integration (merging funds into legitimate economy). Federal statutes 18 U.S.C. §§ 1956 and 1957 criminalize laundering and spending criminally derived funds over $10,000 respectively.', 'criminal', 'Federal', ['RICO', 'Conspiracy', 'Bank Secrecy Act'], ['18 U.S.C. §§ 1956-1957', 'Bank Secrecy Act']],
  ['Civil Asset Forfeiture', 'A legal process by which law enforcement agencies seize property alleged to have been used in or derived from criminal activity, brought as a civil action against the property itself (in rem) rather than against its owner. The government need not charge or convict the property owner — the property is the defendant. Due process requires notice and a hearing but the standard of proof is typically preponderance of the evidence. Equitable sharing allows federal-state profit sharing, creating incentives for aggressive enforcement.', 'criminal', 'Federal/State', ['Criminal Forfeiture', 'Due Process', 'Fourth Amendment'], ['18 U.S.C. § 981', 'United States v. $499,570 in U.S. Currency (2009)']],
  ['Mandatory Minimum Sentencing', 'Statutory provisions requiring judges to sentence convicted defendants to at least a specified period of incarceration, removing judicial discretion to impose lighter sentences regardless of mitigating factors. Federal mandatory minimums are particularly prevalent in drug trafficking, firearms, and child exploitation offenses. The Supreme Court in Apprendi v. New Jersey (2000) and Alleyne v. United States (2013) held that any fact increasing the mandatory minimum must be found by a jury beyond a reasonable doubt.', 'criminal', 'Federal/State', ['Sentencing Guidelines', 'Drug Trafficking', 'Judicial Discretion'], ['Alleyne v. United States (2013)', '21 U.S.C. § 841']],
  ['Habeas Corpus', 'A judicial writ requiring a custodian to bring a detained person before a court to determine the legality of the detention, serving as the fundamental mechanism for challenging unlawful imprisonment. The Great Writ is constitutionally protected against suspension except in cases of rebellion or invasion (Art. I, § 9). Federal habeas corpus for state prisoners (28 U.S.C. § 2254) was significantly constrained by the Antiterrorism and Effective Death Penalty Act (AEDPA) of 1996, which requires deference to state court rulings unless they were unreasonably wrong.', 'criminal', 'Federal/State', ['Due Process', 'Ineffective Assistance of Counsel', 'AEDPA'], ['28 U.S.C. § 2254', 'Boumediene v. Bush (2008)']],

  // ─── CONSTITUTIONAL LAW (EXPANDED) ───────────────────────────────────────
  ['Incorporation Doctrine', 'The constitutional doctrine by which most provisions of the Bill of Rights have been made applicable to the states through the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment. The Supreme Court rejected total incorporation (applying all Bill of Rights provisions to states) in favor of selective incorporation — applying those rights "fundamental to ordered liberty." Almost all Bill of Rights protections have now been incorporated, with the grand jury requirement (5th Amendment) being a notable exception.', 'constitutional', 'Federal', ['Due Process', 'Fourteenth Amendment', 'Substantive Due Process'], ['McDonald v. City of Chicago (2010)', 'Palko v. Connecticut (1937)']],
  ['Strict Scrutiny', 'The highest level of judicial review applied to laws that discriminate based on suspect classifications (race, national origin, alienage) or burden fundamental rights. Under strict scrutiny, the government bears the burden of proving the law is necessary to achieve a compelling governmental interest and is narrowly tailored to achieve that interest. The standard is theoretically exacting — described as "strict in theory and fatal in fact" — though the Court occasionally upholds laws under strict scrutiny.', 'constitutional', 'Federal', ['Intermediate Scrutiny', 'Rational Basis Review', 'Equal Protection'], ['Korematsu v. United States (1944)', 'Adarand Constructors v. Pena (1995)']],
  ['Intermediate Scrutiny', 'A level of judicial review applied to laws that discriminate based on quasi-suspect classifications (sex, legitimacy) or substantially burden important (but not fundamental) interests. The government must show the law is substantially related to achieving an important governmental interest. First articulated in Craig v. Boren (1976) for sex-based distinctions. Unlike strict scrutiny, intermediate scrutiny does not require that the classification be the least restrictive means available.', 'constitutional', 'Federal', ['Strict Scrutiny', 'Rational Basis Review', 'Equal Protection'], ['Craig v. Boren (1976)', 'United States v. Virginia (1996)']],
  ['Substantive Due Process', 'The doctrine that the Due Process Clause protects certain fundamental rights from government interference even when those rights are not expressly mentioned in the Constitution, including privacy, marriage, family relationships, and personal autonomy. The Court has recognized substantive due process rights in contraception (Griswold), interracial marriage (Loving), abortion (now overruled in Dobbs), and same-sex marriage (Obergefell). The Court applies strict scrutiny to laws burdening fundamental rights and rational basis to laws burdening non-fundamental liberties.', 'constitutional', 'Federal', ['Due Process', 'Privacy Rights', 'Incorporation Doctrine'], ['Obergefell v. Hodges (2015)', 'Dobbs v. Jackson Women\'s Health Organization (2022)']],
  ['Commerce Clause', 'Article I, Section 8, Clause 3 of the Constitution granting Congress the power to regulate commerce with foreign nations, among the states, and with Indian tribes. The modern interpretation beginning with Wickard v. Filburn (1942) broadly allows Congress to regulate activities substantially affecting interstate commerce. In United States v. Lopez (1995) and United States v. Morrison (2000), the Court imposed limits on Commerce Clause power, requiring a substantial nexus to interstate commerce. The dormant Commerce Clause restricts states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce.', 'constitutional', 'Federal', ['Supremacy Clause', 'Necessary and Proper Clause', 'Federalism'], ['Wickard v. Filburn (1942)', 'United States v. Lopez (1995)']],
  ['Necessary and Proper Clause', 'Article I, Section 8, Clause 18, granting Congress the power to make all laws necessary and proper for carrying into execution its enumerated powers and all other powers vested in the government. Chief Justice Marshall in McCulloch v. Maryland (1819) broadly interpreted the clause to permit Congress to choose any convenient, useful means to achieve a legitimate end — "let the end be legitimate." The clause is an independent basis for federal legislation and supports many federal regulatory programs not expressly enumerated.', 'constitutional', 'Federal', ['Commerce Clause', 'Enumerated Powers', 'Federalism'], ['McCulloch v. Maryland (1819)', 'U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 18']],
  ['Establishment Clause', 'The First Amendment prohibition against government laws "respecting an establishment of religion," interpreted to require government neutrality toward religion. Tests have varied: the Lemon test (secular purpose, primary effect, no excessive entanglement), the endorsement test, the coercion test, and the historical practices/understanding test most recently applied in Kennedy v. Bremerton School District (2022). Government-sponsored prayer in public schools (Engel v. Vitale, 1962) and invocation-led graduation prayers are prohibited.', 'constitutional', 'Federal', ['Free Exercise Clause', 'Separation of Church and State', 'First Amendment'], ['Engel v. Vitale (1962)', 'Kennedy v. Bremerton School Dist. (2022)']],
  ['Eleventh Amendment Immunity', 'A constitutional provision that bars suits in federal court against a state by citizens of that state, citizens of other states, or foreign nationals. The Supreme Court has interpreted the Eleventh Amendment to embody the broader principle of state sovereign immunity. Congress may abrogate state immunity only pursuant to valid Fourteenth Amendment legislation, not Commerce Clause legislation (Seminole Tribe of Florida v. Florida, 1996). States may waive their immunity by consenting to suit.', 'constitutional', 'Federal', ['Sovereign Immunity', 'Federalism', 'Section 1983'], ['Seminole Tribe of Florida v. Florida (1996)', 'Alden v. Maine (1999)']],
  ['Political Question Doctrine', 'A justiciability doctrine under which federal courts decline to adjudicate matters committed by the Constitution to the political branches (Congress and the President) or that lack judicially manageable standards. The doctrine was analyzed in Baker v. Carr (1962), which identified factors including a textual commitment to another branch, lack of judicially discoverable standards, necessity of nonjudicial policy discretion, and potential for embarrassing multiple pronouncements. Foreign affairs, impeachment proceedings, and partisan gerrymandering (Rucho) have been held nonjusticiable.', 'constitutional', 'Federal', ['Justiciability', 'Separation of Powers', 'Standing'], ['Baker v. Carr (1962)', 'Rucho v. Common Cause (2019)']],
  ['Unconstitutional Conditions Doctrine', 'The principle that the government may not condition the receipt of a government benefit on the waiver of a constitutional right, even though the government is not required to grant the benefit at all. Applying this doctrine, courts have struck down conditions requiring government employees to waive Fourth Amendment rights, conditions requiring welfare recipients to waive privacy rights, and certain conditions on public funding of speech. The doctrine prevents indirect government coercion of constitutional rights through the grant or denial of benefits.', 'constitutional', 'Federal', ['First Amendment', 'Government Benefits', 'Waiver'], ['Perry v. Sindermann (1972)', 'Agency v. Hill (2018)']],

  // ─── CONTRACT LAW (EXPANDED) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Statute of Frauds', 'A doctrine requiring certain contracts to be evidenced by a signed writing to be enforceable, including contracts for the sale of land, contracts not performable within one year, contracts for the sale of goods over $500 (UCC), suretyship contracts, and contracts in consideration of marriage. The writing need not be a formal contract — a signed memorandum, letter, or series of emails may suffice. Part performance, estoppel, and the specially manufactured goods exception can render unwritten contracts enforceable despite the Statute of Frauds.', 'contract', 'Federal/State', ['Promissory Estoppel', 'UCC', 'Parol Evidence Rule'], ['Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 131', 'UCC § 2-201']],
  ['Unconscionability', 'A contract defense allowing courts to refuse to enforce, limit, or modify terms that are unreasonably one-sided, arising from a combination of procedural unconscionability (unfair bargaining process) and substantive unconscionability (unfair terms). Procedural unconscionability includes elements of oppression (unequal bargaining power, no real choice) and surprise (terms buried in fine print). Substantive unconscionability focuses on the harshness or one-sidedness of the contract\'s terms. Both elements are typically required.', 'contract', 'Federal/State', ['Adhesion Contract', 'Duress', 'Unfair Terms'], ['Williams v. Walker-Thomas Furniture Co. (D.C. Cir. 1965)', 'UCC § 2-302']],
  ['Implied Covenant of Good Faith and Fair Dealing', 'An obligation implied in every contract requiring parties to exercise their contractual discretion and perform contractual obligations honestly, fairly, and in a manner consistent with the other party\'s reasonable expectations. In most states, breach gives rise to contract damages; in insurance and employment contexts, some states allow tort damages including punitive damages. The covenant does not override express contract terms but fills gaps and prevents one party from using performance discretion to deprive the other of the benefit of the bargain.', 'contract', 'Federal/State', ['Good Faith', 'Material Breach', 'Breach of Contract'], ['Fortune v. National Cash Register Co. (1977)', 'Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 205']],
  ['Parol Evidence Rule', 'A substantive rule of contract law that bars introduction of extrinsic evidence (oral or written statements made before or contemporaneously with a written contract) to contradict or vary the terms of an integrated written agreement. The rule applies only to prior or contemporaneous statements, not subsequent modifications. Courts admit extrinsic evidence to interpret ambiguous terms, to show conditions precedent, to prove fraud or mistake, or when the writing is not a final integrated agreement.', 'contract', 'Federal/State', ['Statute of Frauds', 'Merger Clause', 'Integration'], ['Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 215', 'UCC § 2-202']],
  ['Force Majeure', 'A contract clause excusing non-performance when an extraordinary event beyond the parties\' control prevents or delays performance, typically listing natural disasters, war, acts of God, government actions, or labor strikes as triggering events. Courts narrowly construe force majeure clauses, requiring that the specific event be listed or clearly within the clause\'s contemplation. COVID-19 litigation tested force majeure clause breadth extensively. Where no force majeure clause exists, parties may invoke impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose defenses.', 'contract', 'Federal/State', ['Impossibility', 'Frustration of Purpose', 'Impracticability'], ['Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 261', 'UCC § 2-615']],
  ['Assignment vs. Delegation', 'An assignment transfers a party\'s rights under a contract to a third party (assignee); a delegation transfers a party\'s duties to a third party (delegate). Rights are generally freely assignable unless the contract prohibits it or assignment would materially change the obligor\'s duty. Duties may be delegated unless a non-assignment clause prohibits it or personal performance is required. Delegation does not relieve the delegator of liability — the original party remains liable unless the obligee agrees to a novation substituting the delegate.', 'contract', 'Federal/State', ['Novation', 'Third-Party Beneficiary', 'Contract Rights'], ['Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 317, 318', 'UCC § 2-210']],

  // ─── TORT LAW (EXPANDED) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Negligence Per Se', 'A doctrine where a defendant\'s violation of a statute or regulation constitutes negligence as a matter of law, provided the plaintiff is in the class of persons the statute was designed to protect and suffered the type of harm the statute was designed to prevent. The plaintiff still must prove causation and damages. Majority jurisdictions treat statutory violation as conclusive evidence of negligence; minority jurisdictions treat it as only evidence of negligence. Common applications include traffic code violations and building code violations.', 'tort', 'State', ['Negligence', 'Duty of Care', 'Res Ipsa Loquitur'], ['Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 14', 'Martin v. Herzog (1920)']],
  ['Res Ipsa Loquitur', 'A doctrine permitting an inference of negligence from the circumstances of an accident without direct proof of a specific negligent act, applying when: the event ordinarily does not occur in the absence of negligence, the instrumentality causing the injury was in the defendant\'s exclusive control, and the plaintiff did not contribute to the accident. Latin for "the thing speaks for itself." Courts debate whether the doctrine creates a rebuttable presumption or merely permits an inference of negligence.', 'tort', 'State', ['Negligence', 'Negligence Per Se', 'Burden of Proof'], ['Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 17', 'Byrne v. Boadle (1863)']],
  ['Products Liability', 'The liability of manufacturers, distributors, and sellers for injuries caused by defective products, involving three defect theories: manufacturing defect (specific unit departs from intended design), design defect (entire line of products is unreasonably dangerous — tested under the consumer expectations or risk-utility test), and failure to warn (inadequate instructions for safe use). Under strict liability, the plaintiff need not prove negligence — only that the product was defective and caused harm. Defenses include assumption of risk, comparative fault, and product misuse.', 'tort', 'State', ['Strict Liability', 'Design Defect', 'Failure to Warn'], ['Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability', 'Greenman v. Yuba Power Products (1963)']],
  ['Intentional Infliction of Emotional Distress (IIED)', 'A tort allowing recovery for severe emotional distress caused by extreme and outrageous conduct intentionally or recklessly aimed at causing such distress. The defendant\'s conduct must exceed all reasonable bounds of decency tolerated in a civilized community. The plaintiff must suffer severe emotional distress, not merely hurt feelings. Physical injury is not required. IIED claims face special scrutiny regarding free speech when the defendant\'s conduct involves expression on matters of public concern (Snyder v. Phelps, 2011).', 'tort', 'State', ['Negligent Infliction of Emotional Distress', 'Outrage', 'Harassment'], ['Restatement (Second) of Torts § 46', 'Snyder v. Phelps (2011)']],
  ['Economic Loss Rule', 'A common law doctrine barring recovery in tort for purely economic losses — lost profits or business opportunities — not accompanied by physical injury to person or property. The rule draws a boundary between tort and contract law, directing parties who suffer only economic loss to seek recovery through contract. Exceptions include fraud, the professional negligence of attorneys and accountants, and products liability for property damage. The rule varies significantly by jurisdiction.', 'tort', 'State', ['Negligence', 'Products Liability', 'Contract Damages'], ['East River S.S. Corp. v. Transamerica Delaval (1986)', 'Restatement (Third) of Torts: Economic Torts']],
  ['Trespass to Land', 'An intentional tort consisting of the unauthorized entry upon another\'s land, or causing an object or third party to enter, regardless of whether the trespasser knows the entry is unauthorized. Unlike negligence, trespass to land requires no physical harm — nominal damages may be recovered. A continuing trespass (leaves object on land) gives rise to successive causes of action. The defendant\'s good faith or reasonable mistake regarding authorization is not a defense to liability, though it may affect damages.', 'tort', 'State', ['Nuisance', 'Property Rights', 'Easement'], ['Restatement (Second) of Torts § 158', 'Black\'s Law Dictionary']],
  ['Private Nuisance', 'An unreasonable and substantial interference with the use and enjoyment of another\'s private land, actionable through tort without requirement of physical trespass. Liability balances the gravity of harm against the utility of the conduct, considering factors such as the extent and duration of the interference, its severity, the social value of the plaintiff\'s use, and the burden of avoiding the harm. Nuisance may be permanent (compensatory damages or injunction) or temporary (abatement plus damages). Public nuisance affects the public at large and may be abated by government action.', 'tort', 'State', ['Trespass to Land', 'Strict Liability', 'Zoning'], ['Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 821A-821F', 'Black\'s Law Dictionary']],

  // ─── PROPERTY LAW (EXPANDED) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Fee Simple Absolute', 'The most complete form of property ownership recognized by law, providing the owner with the greatest possible interest in land — perpetual ownership with the right to use, possess, exclude others, and transfer the property by sale, gift, or devise. No future interest is created in any other person. At common law, a fee simple was conveyed with the language "to A and his heirs" (the words of limitation); modern deeds typically require only a clear expression of intent.', 'property', 'State', ['Life Estate', 'Future Interests', 'Fee Tail'], ['Restatement (Third) of Property: Wills and Other Donative Transfers', 'Black\'s Law Dictionary']],
  ['Life Estate', 'A property interest measured by the life of the holder (life tenant) or another specified person (pur autre vie — for another\'s life), after which the property passes to a remainderman or reverts to the grantor. A life tenant is entitled to possession and use but may not commit "waste" — acts that permanently damage the property and impair the value for the future interest holder. The life tenant owes property taxes and interest on encumbrances but not principal payments.', 'property', 'State', ['Fee Simple Absolute', 'Future Interests', 'Remainderman'], ['Restatement (Third) of Property: Wills and Other Donative Transfers', 'Black\'s Law Dictionary']],
  ['Rule Against Perpetuities', 'A common law rule invalidating future interests that may not necessarily vest within 21 years after the death of some life in being at the time of the grant. The rule prevents "dead hand" control of property in perpetuity and requires that interests vest or fail within the perpetuities period. The traditional common law version is complex and has generated famous conundrums; most states have adopted the Uniform Statutory Rule Against Perpetuities (90-year wait-and-see approach) or abolished the rule entirely.', 'property', 'State', ['Future Interests', 'Remainder', 'Executory Interest'], ['Restatement (Third) of Property: Wills and Other Donative Transfers § 27.1', 'USRAP']],
  ['Implied Warranty of Habitability', 'A landlord\'s implied promise in residential leases that the premises are fit for human habitation throughout the tenancy, covering essential services such as functioning heat, plumbing, electricity, weatherproofing, and freedom from vermin. Modern courts have rejected the traditional rule of caveat emptor in residential leases and recognize this warranty. Tenant remedies for breach include rent withholding (deposit rent in escrow), rent reduction, repair-and-deduct, or lease termination.', 'property', 'State', ['Landlord-Tenant', 'Constructive Eviction', 'Lease'], ['Javins v. First National Realty Corp. (D.C. Cir. 1970)', 'Restatement (Second) of Property: Landlord and Tenant']],
  ['Constructive Eviction', 'The doctrine permitting a tenant to treat a lease as terminated and vacate the premises when the landlord\'s wrongful acts or failures substantially interfere with the tenant\'s use and enjoyment of the property. The landlord\'s breach of the implied warranty of habitability typically constitutes constructive eviction. The tenant must actually vacate within a reasonable time after conditions become intolerable. Once vacated, the tenant is relieved of future rent obligations.', 'property', 'State', ['Implied Warranty of Habitability', 'Lease Termination', 'Landlord-Tenant'], ['Reste Realty Corp. v. Cooper (N.J. 1969)', 'Restatement (Second) of Property: Landlord and Tenant']],
  ['Joint Tenancy vs. Tenants in Common', 'Two forms of co-ownership of property. Joint tenancy requires the four unities (time, title, interest, and possession) and includes the right of survivorship — upon a joint tenant\'s death, their interest automatically passes to surviving joint tenants, avoiding probate. Tenancy in common requires only unity of possession; co-tenants hold separate, transferable, descendible undivided interests with no survivorship right. Joint tenancy may be severed (converted to tenancy in common) by voluntary conveyance of one co-tenant\'s interest.', 'property', 'State', ['Tenancy by the Entirety', 'Survivorship', 'Partition'], ['Restatement (Third) of Property: Wills and Other Donative Transfers', 'Black\'s Law Dictionary']],
  ['Fixture', 'Personal property that has become so affixed or integrated into real property that it is legally considered part of the real estate and passes with a conveyance of the land unless specifically excluded. The test considers the method of annexation, adaptation of the item to the real estate\'s use, and the intention of the annexing party. Trade fixtures installed by tenants for business purposes generally remain personal property and may be removed during the tenancy. Disputes over fixtures commonly arise in real estate transactions, security interests, and landlord-tenant matters.', 'property', 'State', ['Real Property', 'Personal Property', 'Security Interest'], ['Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages', 'Black\'s Law Dictionary']],

  // ─── FAMILY LAW (EXPANDED) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Best Interest of the Child', 'The paramount standard applied in child custody and visitation determinations, requiring courts to consider all factors relevant to the child\'s welfare including the child\'s relationship with each parent, stability of environment, the child\'s wishes (if of sufficient age and maturity), education, health, and history of domestic violence. Every state follows the best interest standard, though the specific factors vary. A rebuttable presumption against custody for domestic violence perpetrators has been adopted in many states.', 'family', 'State', ['Child Custody', 'Parenting Plan', 'Guardian ad Litem'], ['Uniform Marriage and Divorce Act § 402', 'State custody statutes']],
  ['Termination of Parental Rights (TPR)', 'The permanent legal severing of the parent-child relationship by court order, removing all parental rights, duties, and authority, and freeing the child for adoption. Grounds include abuse, neglect, abandonment, mental illness impairing parenting, long-term substance abuse, or conviction of certain crimes against children. Due to the fundamental liberty interest at stake, strict procedural protections apply, including right to appointed counsel in many states (Lassiter v. Dept. of Social Services, 1981). The Adoption and Safe Families Act (1997) requires states to seek TPR after 15 of 22 months in foster care.', 'family', 'Federal/State', ['Child Abuse', 'Adoption', 'Foster Care'], ['Lassiter v. Department of Social Services (1981)', 'Adoption and Safe Families Act of 1997']],
  ['Marital Property (Community Property vs. Equitable Distribution)', 'The two systems governing division of marital property upon divorce. Community property states (Arizona, California, Idaho, Louisiana, Nevada, New Mexico, Texas, Washington, Wisconsin) treat property acquired during marriage as equally owned by both spouses and divide it 50/50. Equitable distribution states (the majority) divide marital property fairly but not necessarily equally, considering factors such as length of marriage, contributions, economic circumstances, and fault in some states. Separate property (acquired before marriage or received as gift/inheritance) generally remains with the owner.', 'family', 'State', ['Divorce', 'Alimony', 'Prenuptial Agreement'], ['State divorce statutes', 'Uniform Marriage and Divorce Act']],
  ['Prenuptial Agreement', 'A contract entered before marriage specifying how property will be divided, debts allocated, and (sometimes) spousal support determined in the event of divorce or death. For enforceability, courts typically require full disclosure of assets, absence of duress, independent legal advice, and procedurally fair circumstances. Under the Uniform Premarital Agreement Act (adopted in many states), prenuptial agreements are enforceable unless unconscionable or executed involuntarily. Provisions attempting to waive child support or establish child custody are unenforceable.', 'family', 'State', ['Postnuptial Agreement', 'Marital Property', 'Divorce'], ['Uniform Premarital Agreement Act', 'State contract law']],
  ['Parens Patriae', 'A Latin doctrine meaning "parent of the nation," providing the basis for state intervention in matters affecting the welfare of children and incompetent persons who cannot care for themselves. The doctrine underlies child protective services, neglect and abuse proceedings, guardianship of incapacitated adults, and commitment of the mentally ill. Courts may exercise parens patriae power to protect minors from their own parents when necessary for the child\'s welfare, subject to constitutional limitations on parental rights.', 'family', 'Federal/State', ['Termination of Parental Rights', 'Guardianship', 'Child Welfare'], ['Prince v. Massachusetts (1944)', 'Parens Patriae doctrine']],
  ['Hague Convention on Child Abduction', 'An international treaty (Hague Convention on the Civil Aspects of International Child Abduction, 1980) to which the United States and 100+ nations are parties, requiring prompt return of children wrongfully removed from or retained outside their country of habitual residence. A wrongful removal occurs when it violates the custody rights of a person who was actually exercising those rights at the time. The return obligation may be refused if return would expose the child to grave risk of harm. The International Child Abduction Remedies Act implements the Convention in U.S. courts.', 'family', 'Federal/International', ['Child Custody', 'International Law', 'Family Law'], ['42 U.S.C. § 11601 et seq.', 'Hague Convention of 1980']],

  // ─── EMPLOYMENT LAW (EXPANDED) ───────────────────────────────────────────
  ['At-Will Employment', 'The default rule in American employment law that employers may terminate employees for any reason (or no reason) not prohibited by law and employees may quit at any time. Exceptions to at-will employment include: statutory protections (Title VII, ADA, FLSA), implied contract exceptions (employee handbook promises), public policy exceptions (refusing to commit crimes), and the implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing in some states. Approximately 70% of American workers are at-will employees.', 'employment', 'State', ['Wrongful Termination', 'Employment Contract', 'NLRA'], ['Payne v. Western & Atlantic Railroad (1884)', 'State employment law']],
  ['Worker Classification', 'The determination of whether a worker is an employee (entitled to minimum wage, overtime, unemployment insurance, workers\' compensation, and NLRA protections) or an independent contractor (generally excluded from those protections). Tests vary by context — the IRS uses a behavioral and financial control plus relationship test; the FLSA uses an "economic reality" test (ABC test in some states); California uses the more stringent ABC test (AB 5) presuming employee status unless all three prongs are met. Misclassification exposing workers to gig economy work arrangements is a major enforcement priority.', 'employment', 'Federal/State', ['FLSA', 'Gig Economy', 'Employee Benefits'], ['Dynamex Operations West v. Superior Court (Cal. 2018)', 'FLSA § 3(d)']],
  ['Hostile Work Environment', 'A form of workplace harassment under Title VII (and parallel state laws) creating a work environment so permeated with discriminatory intimidation, ridicule, and insult that it alters the conditions of employment and creates an abusive working environment. The harassment must be severe or pervasive, objectively offensive, and based on a protected characteristic (race, sex, religion, national origin). Employer liability for supervisor harassment uses a vicarious liability framework with an affirmative defense if the employer had an antiharassment policy and the employee unreasonably failed to report.', 'employment', 'Federal/State', ['Sexual Harassment', 'Title VII', 'Quid Pro Quo'], ['Meritor Savings Bank v. Vinson (1986)', 'Harris v. Forklift Systems Inc. (1993)']],
  ['WARN Act', 'The Worker Adjustment and Retraining Notification Act (1988) requiring employers with 100 or more employees to provide 60 calendar days\' advance notice of plant closings and mass layoffs affecting 50 or more employees at a single site. Failure to provide notice entitles affected employees to back pay and benefits for up to 60 days and civil penalties. Narrow exceptions exist for unforeseeable business circumstances, natural disasters, and "faltering companies." Many states have adopted "mini-WARN Acts" with broader coverage requirements.', 'employment', 'Federal', ['Layoff', 'Plant Closing', 'Employee Rights'], ['29 U.S.C. § 2101 et seq.', 'WARN Act regulations']],
  ['Protected Concerted Activity', 'Employee actions under Section 7 of the National Labor Relations Act protected from employer retaliation, including organizing unions, collective bargaining, and other concerted activities for mutual aid and protection. The protection extends beyond formal union organizing to informal collective action such as group complaints about working conditions, workplace safety concerns, and discussions about wages. The NLRA prohibits employer interference with, restraint of, or coercion of employees exercising Section 7 rights.', 'employment', 'Federal', ['NLRA', 'Unfair Labor Practice', 'Collective Bargaining'], ['29 U.S.C. § 157', 'NLRB v. Washington Aluminum Co. (1962)']],
  ['Non-Compete Agreement', 'A contract in which an employee agrees not to compete with the employer for a specified time period and/or geographic area following termination. Enforceability varies dramatically by state — California, North Dakota, and Minnesota void most non-competes; most other states enforce them if reasonable in scope, duration, and geography. The FTC issued a rule in 2024 banning most non-compete agreements, which faces ongoing legal challenge. Courts commonly "blue pencil" (narrow) overbroad non-competes rather than void them entirely.', 'employment', 'State', ['Trade Secrets', 'Non-Disclosure Agreement', 'Employment Contract'], ['Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 16600', 'FTC Non-Compete Rule (2024)']],
  ['Whistleblower Protection', 'Legal protections prohibiting employer retaliation against employees who report illegal activity, safety violations, or regulatory non-compliance to government authorities or company management. Multiple federal statutes protect whistleblowers in specific sectors: SOX (publicly traded companies), Dodd-Frank (securities), OSHA, False Claims Act, Clean Air Act, and others. The Dodd-Frank Act provides the strongest protections, allowing direct appeals to federal court and awarding 10-30% of successful SEC enforcement actions over $1 million to qualifying informants.', 'employment', 'Federal/State', ['False Claims Act', 'Retaliation', 'SEC Enforcement'], ['Sarbanes-Oxley § 806', 'Dodd-Frank § 922 (15 U.S.C. § 78u-6)']],

  // ─── IMMIGRATION LAW (EXPANDED) ──────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Adjustment of Status', 'The process by which an alien physically present in the United States may obtain lawful permanent resident status ("green card") without leaving the country and obtaining an immigrant visa at a U.S. consulate abroad. Eligibility requires an immediate relative petition or other immigrant visa petition, visa availability (current priority date), and admissibility to the United States. The process is governed by 8 U.S.C. § 1255 and involves USCIS adjudication of Form I-485.', 'immigration', 'Federal', ['Lawful Permanent Resident', 'Visa', 'Immigrant Visa'], ['8 U.S.C. § 1255', 'INA § 245']],
  ['Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals (DACA)', 'An Obama-era executive action (2012) granting prosecutorial discretion — deferred removal proceedings and work authorization — to qualifying individuals brought to the United States as children who meet education or military service requirements, have no significant criminal history, and have continuously resided in the U.S. since 2007. DACA does not provide lawful immigration status or a path to citizenship. Its legal status has been extensively litigated, with the Supreme Court in DHS v. Regents (2020) blocking the Trump administration\'s rescission on administrative procedure grounds.', 'immigration', 'Federal', ['Prosecutorial Discretion', 'Removal Proceedings', 'Work Authorization'], ['DHS v. Regents of the University of California (2020)', 'Executive Branch memoranda']],
  ['Credible Fear Interview', 'A screening process at the border or port of entry for individuals claiming asylum, conducted by an asylum officer to determine whether there is a "significant possibility" that the person can establish eligibility for asylum or protection under the Convention Against Torture. Those who pass are placed in removal proceedings before an immigration judge to fully adjudicate their asylum claim. Those who do not pass may be expeditiously removed but may seek review by an immigration judge. The standard is lower than the ultimate asylum burden of proof.', 'immigration', 'Federal', ['Asylum', 'Expedited Removal', 'Withholding of Removal'], ['8 U.S.C. § 1225(b)(1)(B)', 'INA § 235']],
  ['Aggravated Felony (Immigration)', 'A defined category of offenses under immigration law (8 U.S.C. § 1101(a)(43)) that trigger severe immigration consequences including mandatory detention, deportation without a hearing before an immigration judge in many cases, permanent bar to most forms of relief from removal, and bar to relief including asylum. The term is misleading — it includes offenses that may not be felonies under state law and was dramatically expanded by Congress in 1988, 1990, 1994, and 1996. Conviction of an aggravated felony triggers automatic removal for most noncitizens.', 'immigration', 'Federal', ['Removal', 'Cancellation of Removal', 'Criminal Grounds of Deportability'], ['8 U.S.C. § 1101(a)(43)', 'INA § 101(a)(43)']],
  ['Cancellation of Removal', 'A form of relief from deportation available to qualifying non-permanent residents (10-year continuous presence, good moral character, no aggravated felony, exceptional hardship to qualifying U.S. citizen or LPR family member) and lawful permanent residents (5-year LPR status, 7-year continuous residence, no aggravated felony). Only 4,000 non-LPR cancellations are available per fiscal year (subject to numerical cap). Applicants must appear before an immigration judge and demonstrate all statutory requirements.', 'immigration', 'Federal', ['Removal Proceedings', 'Aggravated Felony', 'Hardship'], ['8 U.S.C. § 1229b', 'INA § 240A']],
  ['Naturalization', 'The legal process by which a foreign citizen or national acquires U.S. citizenship by satisfying statutory requirements including five years of lawful permanent residence (three years if married to a U.S. citizen), continuous residence, physical presence (at least 30 months of the 5-year period), good moral character, ability to speak, read, and write English, and knowledge of U.S. history and government. Applicants must renounce allegiance to foreign states and take an oath of allegiance. USCIS adjudicates naturalization applications.', 'immigration', 'Federal', ['Lawful Permanent Resident', 'Citizenship', 'Path to Citizenship'], ['8 U.S.C. § 1427', 'INA § 316']],

  // ─── BANKRUPTCY LAW (EXPANDED) ───────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Automatic Stay', 'The injunction that arises automatically upon the filing of a bankruptcy petition, immediately halting all debt collection activities against the debtor, including lawsuits, wage garnishments, foreclosures, utility shutoffs, and creditor contact. The stay is one of the fundamental debtor protections in bankruptcy. Creditors who violate the automatic stay may face sanctions including compensatory and punitive damages. The stay may be lifted for cause upon motion, including lack of adequate protection of secured creditors\' interests.', 'bankruptcy', 'Federal', ['Chapter 7', 'Chapter 13', 'Discharge'], ['11 U.S.C. § 362', 'Bankruptcy Reform Act of 1978']],
  ['Preference Payment', 'A transfer of property by an insolvent debtor to a creditor within 90 days before filing bankruptcy (one year for "insider" creditors) on account of an antecedent debt that allows the creditor to receive more than they would in a Chapter 7 liquidation. The bankruptcy trustee may avoid (recover) preferential transfers to redistribute the payment equally among creditors. Defenses include contemporaneous exchange for new value, ordinary course of business payments, and enabling transaction safe harbor.', 'bankruptcy', 'Federal', ['Fraudulent Transfer', 'Trustee Avoiding Powers', 'Automatic Stay'], ['11 U.S.C. § 547', 'In re Roblin Industries (2d Cir. 1992)']],
  ['Fraudulent Transfer (Bankruptcy)', 'A transfer of property made by the debtor with actual intent to hinder, delay, or defraud creditors, or a transfer made for less than reasonably equivalent value when the debtor was insolvent or was rendered insolvent by the transfer. The bankruptcy trustee may avoid fraudulent transfers made within two years before filing under the Bankruptcy Code, or look back further using applicable state fraudulent transfer statutes (Uniform Fraudulent Transfer Act). Ponzi scheme payments received without knowledge of the fraud are protected by the "good faith" safe harbor.', 'bankruptcy', 'Federal', ['Preference Payment', 'Trustee Avoiding Powers', 'Uniform Fraudulent Transfer Act'], ['11 U.S.C. § 548', 'Uniform Voidable Transactions Act']],
  ['Cramdown', 'The ability of a debtor in Chapter 11 (or Chapter 13) to confirm a reorganization plan over the objection of a dissenting class of creditors, provided the plan is "fair and equitable" and does not "discriminate unfairly." Cramdown on a secured creditor requires that the creditor retain its lien and receive deferred cash payments with a present value equal to the collateral\'s value. Absolute priority rule in Chapter 11 requires that junior creditors receive nothing unless senior creditors are paid in full (absent new value contribution).', 'bankruptcy', 'Federal', ['Chapter 11', 'Reorganization Plan', 'Absolute Priority Rule'], ['11 U.S.C. § 1129(b)', 'Till v. SCS Credit Corp. (2004)']],
  ['Discharge (Bankruptcy)', 'The court order at the conclusion of a bankruptcy case that releases the debtor from personal liability for most pre-bankruptcy debts, replacing the debt with an injunction permanently prohibiting creditors from attempting to collect. Discharge does not eliminate non-dischargeable debts: student loans (unless undue hardship), domestic support obligations, certain taxes, debts incurred by fraud, and DUI-related debts. Chapter 7 discharge is granted relatively quickly; Chapter 13 discharge is granted only after completing the repayment plan.', 'bankruptcy', 'Federal', ['Automatic Stay', 'Non-Dischargeable Debt', 'Chapter 7'], ['11 U.S.C. § 727', '11 U.S.C. § 523']],

  // ─── INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY (EXPANDED) ────────────────────────────────────
  ['Copyright Fair Use', 'A statutory defense to copyright infringement (17 U.S.C. § 107) that permits use of copyrighted material without authorization for purposes such as criticism, comment, news reporting, teaching, scholarship, and research. Courts apply a four-factor test: purpose and character of the use (including commerciality and transformativeness), nature of the copyrighted work, amount and substantiality of the portion used, and effect on the market for the original. Transformative works—those adding new meaning, message, or expression—are more likely to constitute fair use (Campbell v. Acuff-Rose Music, 1994).', 'intellectual-property', 'Federal', ['Copyright Infringement', 'DMCA', 'Transformative Use'], ['17 U.S.C. § 107', 'Campbell v. Acuff-Rose Music (1994)']],
  ['DMCA (Digital Millennium Copyright Act)', 'A 1998 federal law implementing two WIPO internet treaties and addressing copyright in the digital environment. Key provisions include: the anti-circumvention prohibition on bypassing technological protection measures (DRM); the safe harbor for online service providers from copyright infringement by their users, conditioned on notice-and-takedown compliance; and prohibitions on copyright management information removal. Section 512 safe harbors require service providers to have a policy for terminating repeat infringers and respond to takedown notices expeditiously.', 'intellectual-property', 'Federal', ['Copyright', 'Fair Use', 'Section 512'], ['17 U.S.C. §§ 512, 1201', 'DMCA (1998)']],
  ['Patent Claim Construction', 'The legal determination of the scope and meaning of patent claims, conducted by the court as a matter of law (Markman v. Westview Instruments, 1996). Intrinsic evidence (the claims, specification, prosecution history) is the primary source for interpretation; extrinsic evidence (dictionaries, expert testimony) is secondary. Claim construction is outcome-determinative in patent cases because infringement analysis requires comparing each element of the asserted claim to the accused product. Claim differentiation presumes that different claim terms have different scopes.', 'intellectual-property', 'Federal', ['Patent Infringement', 'Prosecution History Estoppel', 'Markman Hearing'], ['Markman v. Westview Instruments (1996)', 'Phillips v. AWH Corp. (Fed. Cir. 2005)']],
  ['Trade Secret Misappropriation', 'The improper acquisition, disclosure, or use of a trade secret through theft, bribery, misrepresentation, breach of duty to maintain secrecy, or espionage. The Defend Trade Secrets Act of 2016 (DTSA) created a federal civil cause of action for trade secret misappropriation. Remedies include injunctive relief, unjust enrichment damages or reasonable royalties, and exemplary damages or attorney\'s fees for willful and malicious misappropriation. Court-ordered seizures are available in extraordinary circumstances under the DTSA.', 'intellectual-property', 'Federal/State', ['Non-Disclosure Agreement', 'Non-Compete Agreement', 'UTSA'], ['18 U.S.C. § 1836 et seq.', 'Defend Trade Secrets Act of 2016']],
  ['Trademark Dilution', 'An intellectual property cause of action protecting famous marks from uses that blur the mark\'s distinctiveness or tarnish its reputation, even without competition or consumer confusion. The Federal Trademark Dilution Revision Act of 2006 requires that the mark be famous (widely recognized by the general consuming public) and the use occur after the mark became famous. Dilution by blurring occurs when a similar mark impairs the famous mark\'s distinctiveness; dilution by tarnishment harms the mark\'s reputation through association with unwholesome goods.', 'intellectual-property', 'Federal', ['Trademark Infringement', 'Famous Mark', 'Likelihood of Confusion'], ['15 U.S.C. § 1125(c)', 'Moseley v. V Secret Catalogue Inc. (2003)']],

  // ─── ENVIRONMENTAL LAW (EXPANDED) ────────────────────────────────────────
  ['CERCLA / Superfund', 'The Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Act (1980), which creates a federal program to address releases of hazardous substances at contaminated sites and imposes strict, joint and several, retroactive liability on potentially responsible parties (PRPs) including current and former site owners, operators, and anyone who arranged for disposal. EPA may conduct cleanup and recover costs from PRPs, or may require PRPs to conduct cleanup under a consent decree. CERCLA\'s settlement provisions allow responsible parties to seek contribution from others.', 'environmental', 'Federal', ['EPA', 'Hazardous Waste', 'RCRA'], ['42 U.S.C. § 9601 et seq.', 'Burlington Northern & Santa Fe Railway Co. v. United States (2009)']],
  ['NEPA Environmental Impact Statement', 'The procedural requirement under the National Environmental Policy Act (1970) that federal agencies prepare a detailed Environmental Impact Statement (EIS) before taking any "major Federal action significantly affecting the quality of the human environment." An EIS must analyze the proposed action, alternatives, and environmental consequences. Agencies first prepare an Environmental Assessment (EA); if it concludes no significant impact, a Finding of No Significant Impact (FONSI) is issued instead of an EIS. The Supreme Court in Loper Bright (2024, overruling Chevron) may affect NEPA regulations.', 'environmental', 'Federal', ['NEPA', 'EPA', 'Federal Agencies'], ['42 U.S.C. § 4321 et seq.', 'CEQ NEPA regulations (40 C.F.R. §§ 1500-1508)']],
  ['Endangered Species Act (ESA)', 'A federal statute providing a program for the conservation of threatened and endangered plant and animal species and the ecosystems upon which they depend, administered jointly by the Fish and Wildlife Service and National Marine Fisheries Service. Section 7 prohibits federal agencies from authorizing, funding, or carrying out actions likely to jeopardize the continued existence of listed species or destroy critical habitat. Section 9 prohibits any person from "taking" (including harming the habitat of) listed species. The "God Squad" committee may grant exemptions under limited circumstances.', 'environmental', 'Federal', ['EPA', 'Regulatory Taking', 'Critical Habitat'], ['16 U.S.C. § 1531 et seq.', 'TVA v. Hill (1978)']],
  ['Loper Bright Doctrine (Administrative)', 'The Supreme Court\'s 2024 decision in Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo overruling Chevron U.S.A. Inc. v. Natural Resources Defense Council (1984) and eliminating judicial deference to administrative agencies\' interpretations of ambiguous statutes. Under Chevron, courts deferred to reasonable agency interpretations of ambiguous statutory provisions. Under Loper Bright, courts must independently determine the meaning of federal statutes, using agency interpretations as persuasive but not controlling authority. The decision dramatically increases judicial oversight of administrative law.', 'administrative', 'Federal', ['Chevron Deference', 'Administrative Law', 'Statutory Interpretation'], ['Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo (2024)', 'Chevron U.S.A. Inc. v. NRDC (1984)']],
  ['Clean Air Act NAAQS', 'National Ambient Air Quality Standards, established by EPA under the Clean Air Act, setting maximum concentrations of six "criteria pollutants" (particulate matter, ground-level ozone, carbon monoxide, sulfur dioxide, nitrogen dioxide, and lead) to protect public health and welfare. Primary standards protect public health; secondary standards protect public welfare (crops, buildings, ecosystems). States must adopt State Implementation Plans (SIPs) demonstrating how they will achieve and maintain NAAQS. Areas not meeting standards are "nonattainment areas" subject to enhanced requirements.', 'environmental', 'Federal', ['EPA', 'Clean Air Act', 'State Implementation Plans'], ['42 U.S.C. § 7409', 'Whitman v. American Trucking Associations (2001)']],
  ['Public Trust Doctrine', 'A legal principle requiring the government to hold certain natural resources (navigable waters, tidelands, submerged lands) in trust for public use and benefit, preventing alienation or use that substantially impairs public rights. Tracing to Roman law through English common law, the doctrine has been applied to public access to beaches (California), atmospheric resources (in some climate cases), and wildlife. The Illinois Central Railroad Co. v. Illinois (1892) Supreme Court decision established the doctrine\'s federal application and continues to evolve in state courts regarding climate change.', 'environmental', 'Federal/State', ['Navigation Servitude', 'State Ownership', 'Environmental Rights'], ['Illinois Central Railroad Co. v. Illinois (1892)', 'Marks v. Whitney (Cal. 1971)']],

  // ─── ADMINISTRATIVE LAW (EXPANDED) ───────────────────────────────────────
  ['Notice-and-Comment Rulemaking', 'The standard informal rulemaking procedure under the Administrative Procedure Act (5 U.S.C. § 553) requiring agencies to publish a proposed rule in the Federal Register, accept public comment for a period (nearly always 60 days), consider all significant comments, and publish a final rule with a reasoned response. The final rule must be a logical outgrowth of the proposed rule. Failure to provide adequate notice and comment may result in judicial vacatur of the final rule. Procedural challengers must first exhaust administrative remedies.', 'administrative', 'Federal', ['APA', 'Regulatory Agenda', 'Federal Register'], ['5 U.S.C. § 553', 'Vermont Yankee Nuclear Power Corp. v. NRDC (1978)']],
  ['Arbitrary and Capricious Review', 'The standard of review under APA § 706(2)(A) requiring courts to set aside agency action that is arbitrary, capricious, an abuse of discretion, or otherwise not in accordance with law. Under Motor Vehicle Manufacturers Association v. State Farm (1983), a rule is arbitrary and capricious if the agency: failed to examine the relevant data and articulate a satisfactory explanation; relied on factors Congress did not intend to consider; offered an explanation counter to the evidence; or failed to consider an important aspect of the problem. The standard requires a "reasoned explanation" without being "arbitrary."', 'administrative', 'Federal', ['APA', 'Judicial Review', 'Notice-and-Comment Rulemaking'], ['Motor Vehicle Mfrs. Ass\'n v. State Farm (1983)', '5 U.S.C. § 706(2)(A)']],
  ['Major Questions Doctrine', 'A canon of statutory interpretation requiring Congress to clearly authorize agencies before they may take regulatory actions of vast economic and political significance, to prevent agencies from using ancillary statutory provisions to claim unprecedented regulatory powers. Applied in West Virginia v. EPA (2022) to limit EPA\'s authority to mandate "generation shifting" in the electric power sector under the Clean Air Act. The doctrine is a tool of statutory interpretation, not an independent constitutional limit, but functions as a significant check on expansive agency rulemaking.', 'administrative', 'Federal', ['Loper Bright Doctrine', 'APA', 'Nondelegation'], ['West Virginia v. EPA (2022)', 'FDA v. Brown & Williamson Tobacco Corp. (2000)']],
  ['Exhaustion of Administrative Remedies', 'The doctrine requiring a party to complete available administrative processes and exhaust all administrative review procedures before seeking judicial review of agency action. Exhaustion serves interests of agency autonomy (allowing agencies to correct their own errors), efficiency, and creation of an adequate record for review. Courts may excuse exhaustion where the agency lacks authority to grant relief, the administrative process would be futile, there is urgent need to prevent irreparable harm, or the challenge is to the constitutionality of the agency itself.', 'administrative', 'Federal', ['Administrative Review', 'Primary Jurisdiction', 'Ripeness'], ['McKart v. United States (1969)', '5 U.S.C. § 704']],
  ['Nondelegation Doctrine', 'A constitutional doctrine holding that Congress may not delegate its legislative power to executive agencies without providing an "intelligible principle" to guide the agency\'s exercise of delegated authority. The Supreme Court invalidated agencies under the nondelegation doctrine in 1935 (A.L.A. Schechter Poultry and Panama Refining) but has not done so since, applying a broad interpretation of "intelligible principle." In Gundy v. United States (2019), four justices indicated interest in reinvigorating the doctrine; Loper Bright may further constrain broad delegations.', 'administrative', 'Federal', ['Separation of Powers', 'Major Questions Doctrine', 'APA'], ['A.L.A. Schechter Poultry Corp. v. United States (1935)', 'Gundy v. United States (2019)']],

  // ─── TAX LAW (EXPANDED) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Capital Gains Tax', 'A tax imposed on the profit from the sale of a capital asset (property held outside the ordinary course of business, including stocks, bonds, and real estate). Short-term capital gains (assets held one year or less) are taxed at ordinary income rates; long-term capital gains (assets held more than one year) are taxed at preferential rates of 0%, 15%, or 20% depending on taxable income. Net Investment Income Tax (3.8%) applies under ACA to investment income above certain thresholds. The "step-up in basis" at death eliminates accrued gain on inherited assets.', 'tax', 'Federal', ['Basis', 'Like-Kind Exchange', 'Tax Planning'], ['IRC § 1221-1231', 'IRC § 1(h)']],
  ['Like-Kind Exchange (Section 1031)', 'A tax deferral mechanism allowing taxpayers to defer recognition of capital gains when exchanging "like-kind" real property for another qualifying real property. The tax is not eliminated but deferred until the replacement property is sold in a taxable transaction. The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 limited like-kind exchanges to real property, excluding personal property. Strict timing rules apply: 45 days to identify replacement property and 180 days to close after relinquishing the original property; a qualified intermediary must hold exchange proceeds.', 'tax', 'Federal', ['Capital Gains Tax', 'Basis', 'Real Estate'], ['IRC § 1031', 'Starker v. United States (9th Cir. 1979)']],
  ['Transfer Pricing', 'The prices at which related entities within a multinational enterprise transact with each other for goods, services, and intangibles, subject to tax authority scrutiny to ensure prices reflect the "arm\'s length standard" — prices that unrelated parties would charge in comparable transactions. Under IRC § 482, the IRS may reallocate income between related entities to reflect arm\'s-length pricing. Transfer pricing is the most litigated area of international tax, involving complex comparable analysis and applicable regulations under the OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines.', 'tax', 'Federal/International', ['International Tax', 'BEPS', 'OECD'], ['IRC § 482', 'OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines (2022)']],
  ['Offer in Compromise', 'A program allowing taxpayers to settle their federal tax liability for less than the full amount owed when full payment would create economic hardship or there is doubt as to collectibility or liability. Three grounds: doubt as to collectibility (assets and income can\'t fully pay the tax liability), doubt as to liability (legitimate dispute about the correct tax amount), and effective tax administration (exceptional circumstances make collection unjust). The IRS evaluates reasonable collection potential (assets + monthly income × multiplier) in determining acceptability.', 'tax', 'Federal', ['IRS Collections', 'Tax Debt', 'Installment Agreement'], ['IRC § 7122', 'IRS Form 656']],
  ['Pass-Through Taxation', 'A tax structure in which business income is not taxed at the entity level but "passes through" to the owners and is taxed on their individual returns, avoiding corporate double taxation. Pass-through entities include S corporations, partnerships, LLCs (taxed as partnerships by default), and sole proprietorships. The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 created a 20% deduction (IRC § 199A) for "qualified business income" from pass-through entities. Passive activity loss rules (IRC § 469) limit loss deductions for passive investors in such entities.', 'tax', 'Federal', ['S-Corporation', 'Partnership', 'LLC'], ['IRC § 199A', 'IRC § 469']],

  // ─── CYBERCRIME / TECHNOLOGY LAW (EXPANDED) ──────────────────────────────
  ['Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA)', 'The primary federal computer crime statute (18 U.S.C. § 1030) prohibiting unauthorized access to protected computers to obtain information, cause damage, commit fraud, or threaten extortion. "Exceeds authorized access" is a key — and contested — element; the Supreme Court in Van Buren v. United States (2021) narrowed the concept to access of information one lacks permission to access, not misuse of authorized access. CFAA violations carry criminal penalties and support civil suits for damages. It applies to any computer connected to the internet.', 'cyber', 'Federal', ['Identity Theft', 'Unauthorized Computer Access', 'Cybercrime'], ['18 U.S.C. § 1030', 'Van Buren v. United States (2021)']],
  ['GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation)', 'The European Union\'s comprehensive data protection framework (Regulation (EU) 2016/679) applying to any organization processing personal data of EU residents, regardless of where the organization is located. Key principles include data minimization, purpose limitation, accuracy, storage limitation, and security. Individual rights include access, rectification, erasure ("right to be forgotten"), restriction, portability, and objection. Violations carry fines up to €20 million or 4% of global annual turnover. GDPR has become a global benchmark influencing U.S. state privacy laws.', 'cyber', 'Federal/International', ['CCPA', 'Data Privacy', 'Data Breach'], ['Regulation (EU) 2016/679', 'EU Charter of Fundamental Rights Art. 8']],
  ['CCPA (California Consumer Privacy Act)', 'A California privacy law effective January 2020 that grants California residents the right to know what personal information is collected, the right to delete personal information, the right to opt-out of the sale of personal information, and the right to non-discrimination for exercising these rights. The California Privacy Rights Act (CPRA) of 2020 expanded the CCPA to include the right of correction, restricted use of sensitive personal information, and created the California Privacy Protection Agency. CCPA applies to businesses above size thresholds doing business in California.', 'cyber', 'State', ['GDPR', 'Data Privacy', 'Privacy Rights'], ['Cal. Civ. Code § 1798.100 et seq.', 'California Privacy Rights Act (2020)']],
  ['Section 230 (Communications Decency Act)', 'A federal law (47 U.S.C. § 230) providing that "no provider or user of an interactive computer service shall be treated as the publisher or speaker of any information provided by another information content provider," shielding internet platforms from civil liability for third-party content. Section 230 immunity is broad, covering most content moderation decisions. The provision also immunizes good-faith content removal decisions. Reform proposals to limit § 230 immunity for certain content categories have been extensively debated but not enacted.', 'cyber', 'Federal', ['First Amendment', 'Platform Liability', 'Content Moderation'], ['47 U.S.C. § 230', 'Zeran v. America Online Inc. (4th Cir. 1997)']],
  ['Electronic Surveillance (ECPA)', 'The Electronic Communications Privacy Act (1986) governs government and private interception of wire, oral, and electronic communications and access to stored electronic communications. Title I (Wiretap Act) regulates interception of communications in transit, requiring a court order based on probable cause for law enforcement and prohibiting unauthorized interception. Title II (Stored Communications Act) governs government access to stored email and records held by service providers. Title III (Pen Register) regulates collection of addressing information (metadata).', 'cyber', 'Federal', ['Fourth Amendment', 'CFAA', 'Data Privacy'], ['18 U.S.C. §§ 2510-2523', 'Carpenter v. United States (2018)']],
  ['AI Liability', 'The emerging body of law addressing legal responsibility for harms caused by artificial intelligence systems, involving unsettled questions about whether existing tort, product liability, and contract frameworks adequately address AI-specific risks. Key issues include: liability for AI-generated defamation, products liability for AI design or manufacturing defects, professional liability for AI-assisted medical or legal decisions, discrimination arising from biased AI algorithms (disparate impact under civil rights laws), and autonomous vehicle liability. The EU AI Act (2024) creates a tiered risk-based regulatory framework.', 'cyber', 'Federal/International', ['Products Liability', 'Section 230', 'Algorithmic Bias'], ['EU AI Act (2024)', 'Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability']],

  // ─── INTERNATIONAL LAW (EXPANDED) ────────────────────────────────────────
  ['Jus Cogens', 'Peremptory norms of general international law from which no derogation is permitted, recognized by the international community as norms so fundamental that they override conflicting treaty obligations. Jus cogens norms include the prohibitions on genocide, torture, slavery, and crimes against humanity, as well as the principle of sovereign equality. A treaty that conflicts with a jus cogens norm is void under the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties (VCLT), Article 53. The content and consequences of jus cogens norms are debated in international legal scholarship.', 'international', 'International', ['Customary International Law', 'Crimes Against Humanity', 'Genocide'], ['Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties (1969) Art. 53', 'ICJ Statute Art. 38']],
  ['State Responsibility', 'The body of international law governing the conditions under which a state is internationally responsible for wrongful acts attributable to it and the legal consequences of that responsibility. The ILC Articles on State Responsibility (2001) codify when conduct is attributable to a state (including acts of state organs and non-state actors the state directs or controls), when an internationally wrongful act has occurred, and the remedies owed (cessation, restitution, compensation, satisfaction). Circumstances precluding wrongfulness include consent, self-defense, force majeure, necessity, and countermeasures.', 'international', 'International', ['Customary International Law', 'ICJ', 'Diplomatic Immunity'], ['ILC Articles on State Responsibility (2001)', 'ICJ Statute']],
  ['Foreign Sovereign Immunities Act (FSIA)', 'The federal statute (28 U.S.C. § 1602) providing that foreign states are immune from suit in U.S. courts as a general rule, but establishing significant exceptions including commercial activities carried out in or having direct effects in the United States, cases involving expropriation of property, terrorist state exceptions for certain claims, and waiver. The FSIA exclusively governs foreign sovereign immunity in U.S. courts. Jurisdiction over foreign states requires serving process on the sovereign directly through diplomatic channels or other approved methods.', 'international', 'Federal', ['Sovereign Immunity', 'Diplomatic Immunity', 'ICSID'], ['28 U.S.C. §§ 1602-1611', 'Republic of Argentina v. NML Capital Ltd. (2014)']],
  ['UNCLOS (Law of the Sea)', 'The United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (1982), defining the rights and responsibilities of nations regarding their use of the world\'s oceans. Key provisions establish: the territorial sea (12 nautical miles, full sovereignty), the exclusive economic zone (200 nautical miles, sovereign rights over resources), the continental shelf, freedom of navigation on the high seas, deep-seabed mining regimes, and compulsory dispute resolution. The United States has not ratified UNCLOS but considers many provisions to reflect customary international law.', 'international', 'International', ['Maritime Law', 'Territorial Waters', 'Freedom of Navigation'], ['UNCLOS (1982)', 'South China Sea Arbitration (2016)']],
  ['WTO Dispute Resolution', 'The World Trade Organization\'s binding dispute settlement system, the cornerstone of the international trading order, allowing member states to challenge other members\' trade measures as inconsistent with WTO agreements. The process involves consultations, a panel report, and appellate review by the Appellate Body — though the Appellate Body has been non-functional since 2019 due to U.S. blocking of appointments. Panel and Appellate Body reports may authorize retaliation (suspension of concessions) against non-complying members.', 'international', 'International', ['Trade Law', 'GATT', 'Retaliation'], ['WTO Dispute Settlement Understanding (DSU)', 'Marrakesh Agreement Establishing the WTO (1994)']],
  ['Bilateral Investment Treaty (BIT)', 'An agreement between two countries establishing terms for private investment by nationals and companies of each country in the other\'s territory, providing protections including national treatment, most-favored-nation treatment, fair and equitable treatment, protection from expropriation without compensation, and free transfer of funds. Most BITs include investor-state dispute settlement (ISDS) provisions allowing foreign investors to bring arbitral claims directly against host states. The United States has approximately 40 BITs in force.', 'international', 'Federal/International', ['ICSID Arbitration', 'State Responsibility', 'Expropriation'], ['UNCTAD Investment Policy Framework', 'ICSID Convention']],

];

